## Jackknife+ conformal prediction

**Jackknife+** is the most expensive method. It refits the GAM $n$ times, each time leaving out one
observation (leave-one-out). This produces the most precise residual distribution and typically
yields the tightest intervals with the best empirical coverage.

The computational cost scales linearly in $n$ (one model refit per observation), which makes
jackknife+ impractical for very large datasets. For moderate-sized data ($n < 2000$), it is often
the best choice.

::: {#69649b83 .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Use a smaller dataset for jackknife+ (LOO refits are expensive)
n_small = 150
x_small = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, n_small)
y_small = np.sin(x_small) + 0.3 * rng.normal(0, 1, n_small)

predictor_jk = wk.conformal_fit(
    "y ~ s(x)",
    {"x": x_small, "y": y_small},
    method="jackknife+",
    level=0.95,
    seed=42,
)

result_jk = predictor_jk.predict({"x": x_new})

print(f"Jackknife+ interval width (mean): {(result_jk.upper - result_jk.lower).mean():.4f}")
```

